#!/bin/sh
# Runs INSIDE the Terminal window the applet opens.  $1 = course directory, $2 = window fifo.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE SYNCHRONISATION, and every token in it was measured (#134). Three agents
# audited an earlier design where the fd was opened by the typed command instead; that version
# could block the waiter forever and returned a false verdict under tcsh. What survived:
#
#   * THE FD IS OPENED HERE, not typed at the student's interactive shell. Typed there, any job
#     the student backgrounds inherits the write end and the applet waits on it forever -- and
#     `./cs193v 9>&-` cannot defend that, because the leak is upstream of the launcher. Measured
#     on real interactive zsh and bash, with a positive control.
#   * `9<>` AND NOT `9>`: read-write opens without blocking even with no reader yet, so the
#     rendezvous cannot strand this window before the launcher starts.
#   * `9>&-` ON THE LAUNCHER: it must not inherit the write end, or a lingering `podman exec`
#     child holds the pipe open and the applet's EOF never arrives. cs193v-ui.sh:519 records the
#     same hazard for conmon in the launcher's own fifo protocol.
#   * THE SENTINEL IS GATED ON THE EXIT STATUS. Writing it unconditionally would close the window
#     ~100ms after a fast refusal -- session-in-use, the foreign-directory box -- and the student
#     would never read the error. On failure we write nothing, the applet sees EOF with no
#     verdict, and it leaves the window open.
# THE FIFO IS OPENED FIRST, BEFORE ANYTHING THAT CAN FAIL, and the order is the whole point.
# The waiter's `read -r v < fifo` blocks in open(2) until a writer appears, so every line above
# this one is a region where a failure here strands the waiter FOREVER -- it never becomes a
# writer, EOF never arrives, and the applet hangs holding a Dock icon. Worse, a live applet makes
# every later launch a silent no-op (macOS sends `reopen` to the running instance, which a blocked
# applet cannot dispatch -- see #274), so the app is bricked until the process is killed.
#
# MEASURED, NOT THEORISED: with `cd` above this line, pointing the course-dir record at a folder
# the student had moved left the applet blocked for 15 minutes on a fifo with ZERO holders. The
# synchronisation review missed it because it only examined what happens after the launcher runs.
#
# `9<>` so the open cannot block, and it is read-write so this process alone keeps the pipe alive
# until it exits -- whereupon fd 9 closes, the waiter reads EOF, and an unsuccessful run correctly
# leaves its tab open. What remains uncovered is only a failure to START this script at all; it
# lives inside the signed bundle, so that means a broken bundle rather than a student's mistake.
exec 9<>"$2"
cd "$1" || exit 1
./cs193v 9>&-
st=$?
[ "$st" -eq 0 ] && printf 'ok\n' >&9
exec 9>&-
exit $st
